@node Keys in a Lean buffer
@section Keys in a Lean buffer

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{C-c C-i}
@tab Show or hide the goal display
@tab @code{lean4-toggle-info}
@item @samp{C-c C-s}
@tab Pin the goal display to point, or unpin it
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pin}
@item @samp{C-c C-SPC}
@tab Pause or unpause the goal display
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pause}
@item @samp{C-c C-k}
@tab Echo the keystroke needed to input the symbol at point
@tab @code{quail-show-key}
@item @samp{C-c C-d}
@tab Recompile and reload imports
@tab @code{lean4-refresh-file-dependencies}
@item @samp{C-c C-r}
@tab Restart the language server
@tab @code{eglot-reconnect}
@item @samp{C-c C-x} or @samp{C-c C-l}
@tab Execute Lean in stand-alone mode
@tab @code{lean4-std-exe}
@item @samp{C-c C-p C-l}
@tab Build the package with Lake
@tab @code{lean4-lake-build}
@item @samp{TAB}
@tab Indent, cycling through the alternatives when pressed again
@tab @code{lean4-tab-indent}
@end multitable

@node Keys in the goal display
@section Keys in the goal display

Goals arrive as trees in which every subterm is labelled, so point can
rest on any part of a goal and ask about it.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{M-.} or @samp{RET}
@tab Jump to the definition of the subterm at point
@tab @code{xref-find-definitions}
@item @samp{C-c C-t}
@tab Jump to the definition of its type
@tab @code{lean4-info-goto-type-definition}
@item @samp{TAB}
@tab Fold or unfold the trace or section at point
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-fold}
@item @samp{C-c C-s}
@tab Pin the display to point, or unpin it
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pin}
@item @samp{C-c C-SPC}
@tab Pause or unpause the display
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pause}
@end multitable

The last two are bound here as well as in the Lean buffer, so the
display can be pinned or paused without leaving it.

The type and docstring of the subterm at point are reported through
ElDoc, so they appear wherever ElDoc is configured to put them: the
echo area by default, or a child frame with a package such as
@samp{eldoc-box}.

Traces fold.  Lean does not send the children of a trace node until
something asks for them -- a @samp{simp} trace on a real proof is enormous --
so unfolding one fetches it.

@node Errors and warnings
@section Errors and warnings

Diagnostics are reported through Flymake, which Eglot drives.  @samp{C-c ! n}
and @samp{C-c ! p} move between them, and @code{flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics}
lists them.

Messages appear in the goal display placed by Lean's own notion of
which declaration they belong to, so a message about a multi-line
declaration stays visible while point moves through it.  Terms inside a
message are as interactive as terms inside a goal.

@node Completion
@section Completion

Completion is offered through @code{completion-at-point-functions}, so any
completion interface works: the default @samp{*Completions*} buffer,
@samp{company}, @samp{corfu} or anything else.  Two sources are active in a Lean
buffer -- Eglot's, and one for the Unicode abbreviations, so that typing
@samp{\\al} offers @samp{\\alpha} and inserting it produces α.

Typing an abbreviation in full still works without completion: the Lean
input method replaces it as soon as it is unambiguous.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

Lean4-Mode uses @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/eglot/, Eglot}, the LSP client built into Emacs 29 and later, so
Eglot's own configuration applies.

@menu
* Frequently used options::
* Semantic highlighting::
* Optional packages::
@end menu

@node Frequently used options
@section Frequently used options

@itemize
@item
@code{lean4-auto-start-server} (default @samp{t}) -- whether visiting a Lean
file starts the language server.  Set to @samp{nil} to start it by hand
with @samp{M-x eglot}.
@item
@code{lean4-info-auto-open} (default @samp{t}) -- whether the goal display
appears when a Lean file is opened, as VS Code's InfoView does.
@item
@code{lean4-info-interactive} (default @samp{t}) -- whether to fetch goals over
Lean's interactive RPC@.  With @samp{nil}, goals are plain text and cannot
be hovered or jumped from.
@item
@code{lean4-indent-offset} (default @samp{2}) -- columns per level of
indentation.
@item
@code{lean4-indent-function} -- @code{lean4-indent-line} guesses from the layout
and cycles when pressed again; @code{eri-indent} does not guess and only
cycles, which is what this mode did before version 2.
@item
@code{lean4-enable-file-watchers} (default @samp{nil}) -- whether to honour the
server's request to watch every @samp{*.ilean} file.  On a project the size
of Mathlib that is tens of thousands of files.
@item
@code{lean4-server-arguments} -- extra arguments for the language server.
@item
@code{lean4-workspace-roots} and @code{lean4-workspace-exclusions} -- directories
to treat as workspace roots, and directories in which never to start a
server.
@end itemize

@node Semantic highlighting
@section Semantic highlighting

Lean classifies identifiers during elaboration, and reports the result
as LSP semantic tokens.  Eglot gained support for these in version
1.20, which is newer than the Eglot bundled with Emacs 29 (1.12) or
Emacs 30 (1.17).  Lean4-Mode uses it when it is available and says
nothing when it is not, so

@example
M-x package-install RET eglot RET
@end example


turns on semantic highlighting on any supported Emacs.

@node Optional packages
@section Optional packages

None of these are required, and none are declared as dependencies.

@itemize
@item
@samp{markdown-mode} -- renders docstrings in hover popups.
@item
@samp{eldoc-box} -- shows ElDoc output in a child frame rather than the
echo area.
@item
@samp{company} or @samp{corfu} -- a completion interface.
@item
@samp{consult-eglot} -- searching workspace symbols.
@end itemize

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@samp{M-x lean4-diagnose} reports how things are set up: the versions of
Emacs and of each dependency, the Lean and Lake executables it found,
the workspace root and its toolchain, the command used to start the
server and whether one is running, and the diagnostics currently in the
buffer with Lean's own fields on each.  It reports on a Lean buffer even
when run from somewhere else.

Include its output when reporting a problem; the counterpart in VS Code
is "Troubleshooting: Show Setup Information".

@node Common Pitfalls
@chapter Common Pitfalls

Lean4-Mode only supports version 4 of Lean.  For editing Lean version
3, use @uref{https://github.com/leanprover/lean3-mode, Lean3-Mode}, which is also known as Lean-Mode due to historical
reasons.  In principle, it is fine to have both Lean3-Mode and
Lean4-Mode installed at the same time.  But note that Lean3-Mode uses
the prefix @samp{lean-} for its symbols.  E.g. you should not use
@samp{lean-}-prefixed commands in a buffer with Lean4-Mode as major mode.

